{
  "abstract": "We prove located source counts and additive-structure restrictions for narrow Collatz cycles. Exact ternary pair matching gives every height-one source exponent above $0.4333973955\\ldots$ near the critical slope. Cycle slope errors are consequently $O(u^{-\\mu})$ for every $2<\\mu<2.3073511985\\ldots$; prior coprimality gives $O(\\log\\log U)$ possible periods below $U$. At height two, a mechanical decomposition of the actual bridge law gives source count $O(Y^{131/200})$ and $O(U^{62/131})$ possible periods without a quantitative irrationality measure. Union over slopes and disjoint cycle states, using the explicitly imported irrationality measure three, give $O_\\varepsilon(U^{193/200+\\varepsilon})$ height-two cycles and $O_\\varepsilon(U^{0.3001921865\\ldots+\\varepsilon})$ height-one cycles. Exact clipping with all terminal lifts treats sparse sets of arbitrarily high defects, with source powers $11/25$ and $33/50$ above levels one and two. Physical-band visits are at most $CY^\\theta$ plus $O(\\log Y)$ per exit, with the corresponding narrow-band powers. A different consequence of pair matching identifies arbitrary selected numerator families with Boolean-cube subsets. Combining Kane–Tao's cube energy theorem with a paid transfer to actual source integers yields $E(A)\\ll[\\log(2u)]^3|A|^{\\log_2 6}$ for every subset of a height-one cycle's full shortcut state set, and polynomial sumset growth of power $\\log_2(8/3)$. Seven exact audits check finite interfaces. Peer-check scopes are explicit. Source-count onsets remain ineffective; the finite energy transfer is effective. No finite cycle record, finiteness theorem or general convergence claim is made.",
